---
scope: src/tools/upgrade
intent: >
  aide_upgrade is the explicit "bring this host project up to date with
  canonical" tool. It exists because aide_init is idempotent and never
  overwrites existing files — which protects customizations but means
  host projects get stale when canonical docs, commands, or the pointer
  stub are updated upstream. aide_upgrade closes that gap with an
  agent-driven, interactive flow: the tool performs a dry-run
  comparison of every driftable artifact against canonical and returns
  structured results the agent presents to the user per-category. The
  agent walks through each category — methodology docs, slash commands,
  pointer stub, agents, skills, MCP config, IDE config — showing what
  would change and letting the user confirm before anything is written.

  MCP config changes follow the prescription pattern: the tool returns
  the canonical server entry, and the agent reads the existing config,
  merges the entry, and writes. If the config is malformed JSON, the
  agent tells the user and asks how to proceed instead of clobbering.
  This eliminates the only destructive path in the prior design.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - The tool always returns a structured JSON response — never a
      pre-formatted string — containing per-file comparison results
      grouped by category. Each result includes the file path, the
      comparison status (matches, differs, missing, malformed), and
      for MCP config the prescription data (key name + entry object).
    - The agent presents results per-category and asks the user which
      categories to apply. The user can confirm all, confirm some,
      or decline. Per-file granularity within a category is not
      offered — a category is all-or-nothing to prevent mixed-version
      artifacts within the same category.
    - For MCP config, the tool returns the canonical aide server entry
      as a prescription. The agent reads the existing config, compares
      the aide entry, and if it differs, shows the user what would
      change and merges on confirmation. Other servers are untouched.
    - When the existing MCP config is malformed JSON, the tool reports
      "malformed" status instead of clobbering. The agent surfaces
      this to the user and asks how to proceed.
    - Files outside the methodology surface are never touched. User
      code, user `.aide` specs, and any file not installed by
      aide_init are invisible to this tool.
    - After applying, each file is reported with a final status:
      updated (content differed and was replaced), unchanged (already
      matched canonical), or created (was missing). The agent
      composes its own summary from the structured data.
  undesired:
    - An upgrade that silently overwrites files without presenting
      the dry-run results to the user first. The agent-driven model
      requires user interaction before any write.
    - An upgrade that returns a pre-formatted prose summary instead
      of structured data. The agent needs structured JSON to drive
      the interactive per-category flow.
    - An upgrade that applies all categories at once without giving
      the user per-category control. The user may want to update
      methodology docs but keep their customized slash commands.
    - An upgrade that clobbers the MCP config when it encounters
      malformed JSON. The agent handles this case by asking the user.
    - An upgrade that touches user code, user `.aide` specs, or
      any file outside the methodology surface.
    - A dry-run that modifies files on disk. The comparison phase is
      read-only; any side effect during comparison is a bug.
    - A dry-run whose warning is vague ("some files may change")
      instead of naming the specific categories and files affected.
---

## Context

aide_init is deliberately conservative: if a file exists, it stays. That
invariant protects every customization the user has made to slash commands,
methodology docs, even the pointer stub. But it also means a host project
initialized six months ago still carries the six-month-old versions of
every artifact init installed. When a canonical doc is rewritten, a command
template is expanded, or the pointer stub learns a new teaching sentence,
init has no path to deliver the update — re-running it just reports
"already exists" for every step. The host drifts silently, and the drift
compounds with every upstream improvement.

aide_upgrade is the controlled update that closes this gap. It
deliberately does what init deliberately refuses to do: replace drifted
methodology artifacts with the current canonical version. The two tools
form a complementary pair — init is safe to run without thinking, upgrade
requires the user's informed consent.

The original design used a two-phase dry-run-then-confirm model where
the tool executed everything on `confirm: true`. This had two problems:
the confirm was all-or-nothing (no per-category control), and the MCP
config path had a destructive edge case where malformed JSON caused a
full overwrite of the file, destroying other servers. The agent-driven
model fixes both: the agent presents results per-category so the user
can selectively confirm, and MCP changes are prescriptions the agent
merges rather than writes the tool fires.

The upgrade surface is exactly the set of files init installs — pipeline
slash commands, methodology docs, the hub index, the pointer stub, agent
definitions, skill templates, MCP wiring, IDE configuration. User code
and user `.aide` specs are outside the surface and invisible to the tool.
This boundary is load-bearing: upgrade must feel safe to confirm precisely
because its scope is narrow and predictable.

## Strategy

**The tool is a comparator; the agent is the executor.** aide_upgrade
reads every canonical artifact, compares it against the host version,
and returns structured results grouped by category. The agent presents
each category to the user and applies only the categories the user
confirms. The tool never writes files during comparison.

**Per-category control replaces all-or-nothing confirm.** The agent
presents results grouped into: pointer stub, methodology docs, version
metadata, slash commands, agents, skills, MCP config, IDE config. The
user can confirm any subset. Within a category, all files are updated
together — no per-file opt-out — to prevent mixed-version artifacts.

**Status is determined by byte comparison, not by file presence alone.**
The comparison reads canonical content from the bundled docs, reads the
host file from disk, and compares bytes. Missing files are "would create".
Matching files are "unchanged". Differing files are "would update". No
diffing algorithm is needed — the tool reports *that* files differ, and
the agent can show diffs using its own tools if the user wants them.

**MCP config is a prescription, not a write.** The tool returns the
canonical aide server entry. The agent reads the existing MCP config,
compares the aide key, and if it differs, shows the user the current
vs canonical entry and merges on confirmation. Malformed JSON is
reported as a status — the agent asks the user, not the tool.

**The pointer stub is spliced within marker boundaries.** The stub
lives inside a larger config file that contains user content outside
the markers. The replacement targets only the marker-bounded region,
leaving everything before and after untouched.

**IDE configuration follows the same per-category flow.** The agent
asks whether the user wants Zed and/or VS Code updates applied.

## Good examples

A developer runs `aide_upgrade`. The agent responds:

> I compared your project against the latest AIDE canonical. Here's
> what's drifted:
>
> **Methodology docs** (2 of 7 differ):
>   ~ .aide/docs/aide-template.md: would update
>   ~ .aide/docs/automated-qa.md: would update
>
> **Slash commands** (1 of 8 differs):
>   ~ aide:research: would update
>
> **Everything else matches** — pointer stub, agents, skills, MCP
> config, IDE config are all current.
>
> Want me to update the methodology docs? The slash commands? Both?

The user says "just the docs for now." The agent applies only the
methodology docs category and reports the result.

A developer's `.mcp.json` has a syntax error. The agent responds:

> Your .mcp.json has a JSON syntax error — I can't compare the aide
> server entry against canonical. Want me to show you the file so you
> can fix it, or would you like me to help repair it?

No data is lost. The user decides.

A fully-current project returns all "unchanged" results. The agent
says:

> All 25 methodology artifacts match canonical. Nothing to upgrade.

## Bad examples

An upgrade that fires `confirm: true` and replaces every artifact in
one shot. The user customized three slash commands; they're gone, and
the only recovery is `git checkout`. Per-category control exists to
prevent this.

An upgrade that encounters malformed `.mcp.json` and writes a fresh
config with only the aide entry, destroying the user's other servers.
The tool decided for them. The correct behavior is to report the
malformed status and let the agent ask.

An upgrade that returns a formatted multi-line string the agent prints
verbatim. The agent cannot drive per-category confirmation, cannot
interleave questions, cannot tailor its output. The structured-data
contract was violated.
